---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pattern = [
  0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 0, 1,
  2, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0,
];
---

<Site>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="live-dot"></span> LOGIC · SIGNALS · CONNECTIONS
      </p>
      <h1>
        Small lights.
        <br />
        <em>Big ideas.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="intro">
        KajLights is an experimental project for learning and making with
        programmable lights, sound and small connected devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn a few lines of code into a moving colour pattern. Change a rule and
        see what happens. Start in your browser, then take your ideas into the
        physical world when you’re ready.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button dark" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
          Try without a board <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/`}>
          Build your first lights →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="light-study">
      <div class="study-label">
        <span>01 / A LITTLE SYSTEM</span>
        <span>INPUT → RULE → LIGHT</span>
      </div>
      <div
        class="led-grid"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A repeating light pattern on a six by six LED grid"
      >
        {pattern.map((n, i) => (
          <span class={`led level-${n}`} style={`--i:${i}`}></span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        A simple rule can make a surprising pattern.
        <br />
        The interesting part is finding out why.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <aside class="wrap" aria-labelledby="early-testing">
    <div class="testing-note">
      <p class="eyebrow">A WORKSHOP IN PROGRESS</p>
      <h2 id="early-testing">Early testing. A few rough edges included.</h2>
      <p>
        KajLights is still a proof of concept. Expect bugs, missing features and
        gaps in the instructions — sometimes all three at once. Things will
        change, and a board may need wiping and setting up again. This is a
        place to experiment, rather than a finished kit or a dependable
        home-lighting system just yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Something confusing or broken?{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/KajLights.github.io/issues">
          Tell us what you tried
        </a>
        . Finding the rough edges helps make the next person’s first steps
        easier.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
  <section class="wrap section split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">WHY LIGHTS?</p>
      <h2>Make computing something you can see.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        A loop becomes a chase of lights. A changing number becomes a colour. A
        message from another device becomes something you can notice. The aim is
        to make programming, electronics and connected systems approachable
        through small experiments with visible results.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightVM is the lighting part of the project: write patterns using
        Python, JavaScript or visual blocks, preview them on screen, and run
        them on a small controller. The companion kaj-fi project explores sound
        analysis and sharing audio features between devices; its public firmware
        is not yet available.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can play with an example, follow a lesson or make something of your
        own. You don’t need a board or an account to try the browser lighting
        playground. Teachers and curious makers are welcome too; the learning
        material is still being developed and tested.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">ROOM TO PLAY AND MAKE</p>
      <h2>
        Something to make.
        <br />
        Something to figure out.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="subjects">
      <article>
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <h3>Computing logic</h3>
        <p>
          Patterns, choices, repetition and state. Change the instructions and
          follow the result.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
          Open the light editor ↗
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <h3>Hardware & signals</h3>
        <p>
          See how a small board meets the physical world through pins, timing,
          light and sound.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}hardware/`}>
          Meet the boards →
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <h3>Connected systems</h3>
        <p>
          Give a device a name, discover what it can do, and investigate the
          messages between things.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>
          Connect a device →
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section split" id="what-is-a-board">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">FROM SCREEN TO REAL LIGHTS</p>
      <h2>What’s a board, anyway?</h2>
      <p>
        A board is a small circuit board with a programmable chip called a
        microcontroller, a USB socket and connection pins. It runs your lighting
        program and sends instructions to the LEDs. The LED strip is a separate
        part: the board is the controller, and the lights show the result.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Buy the board. Make the project.</h3>
      <p>
        You don’t need to design a circuit board from scratch. Start with an
        off-the-shelf Raspberry Pi Pico W or Pico 2 W, or one of the supported
        Seeed XIAO ESP32 models. You can buy these from electronics suppliers;
        our hardware guide links to the manufacturers and their retailers.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Build your own” means connecting that board to a few LEDs and
        installing LightVM, the software that runs on it. Choose the exact model
        in the guide and check firmware availability before buying: not every
        board with a similar name can use the same software.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}hardware/#find-a-board`}>
          Find a board →
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/#parts`}>
          See the starter parts list →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="setup-band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">BUILD YOUR OWN BOARD SETUP</p>
        <h2>
          Your first
          <br />
          little light project.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Start small: a supported board, a USB data cable, a short addressable
          LED strip or ring, connecting wires and suitable power. Our build
          guide covers the complete parts list, signal components and first
          connections.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="journey">
        <li>
          <span>1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Gather the bits</h3>
            <p>
              Choose an exact board model and check its firmware options. Use
              parts with fitted headers and leads if you want less soldering.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Connect and set up</h3>
            <p>
              Follow the wiring guide, install matching firmware, and tell
              LightVM which pin and how many LEDs you connected.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Make something glow</h3>
            <p>
              Try a simple colour at low brightness. Then change a pattern,
              explore a lesson, or invent your own animation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap actions">
      <a class="button dark" href={`${base}build/`}>
        Parts and build guide →
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>
        Already running KajLights? Open your devices →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>
